---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'How Private Background Removal Works | Local Cutout';
const description =
  'See how Local Cutout validates, resizes, and removes an image background entirely inside your web browser.';
---

<BaseLayout {title} {description} path="/how-it-works/">
  <article class="shell prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Behind the cutout</p>
    <h1>How browser-only background removal works</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Local Cutout turns a photo into a transparent PNG without sending the photo to a server.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Your browser checks the file</h2>
    <p>
      The tool accepts PNG, JPG, and WebP images up to 20 MB. It checks the file contents—not just
      the extension—then refuses images over 40 megapixels before processing them.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Large images are resized</h2>
    <p>
      Images wider or taller than 2,000 pixels are scaled down in memory. This limits processing time
      and memory use while keeping enough detail for most product photos, profile pictures, and stickers.
    </p>

    <h2>3. A local model finds the subject</h2>
    <p>
      The browser downloads the Apache-2.0 licensed MODNet model from Hugging Face. A Web Worker runs
      the model away from the page’s main thread, so controls and scrolling remain responsive.
    </p>

    <h2>4. The result becomes a PNG</h2>
    <p>
      The model returns pixels with a transparent background. A canvas previews those pixels on a
      checkerboard, white, or black background. When you download, the browser encodes the canvas as
      a PNG with its alpha channel intact.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Difficult edges can get a second pass</h2>
    <p>
      On browsers with WebGPU, Improve accuracy can download the larger BiRefNet Lite model and
      reprocess the same local pixels. This optional pass is intended for hair, fur, low contrast,
      and complex objects. Restore and Erase remain available for final corrections.
    </p>

    <p><a class="button" href="/">Make a cutout</a></p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
